---
import Layout from '../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
import { GITHUB_URL, LINKEDIN_URL } from '../consts';
---

<Layout
	title="About"
	description="Senior RAN engineer (15+ years, 2G–5G) building AI agents for network operations. Open to Telecom AI, RAN automation and AI engineering roles."
	pubDate={new Date('2026-10-03T12:00:00-05:00')}
>
	<p>
		I'm <strong>Gino Viloria</strong>, a telecommunications engineer from Barranquilla, Colombia. I
		build <strong>AI agents for network operations</strong>: assistants that read network KPIs and
		configuration, find the root cause of a problem and explain it with evidence, so engineers
		spend their time deciding instead of searching.
	</p>

	<h2>What I bring</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>15+ years in 2G–5G radio access networks</strong> across Colombia, Ecuador, Peru,
			Argentina, Mexico, Guatemala, Nicaragua and Japan, including the RF team that launched
			Rakuten Mobile's Open RAN network in Tokyo, the first fully cloud-native commercial network.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Automation that runs in production.</strong> At WOM Colombia I built a parameter audit
			framework for ~30K Huawei cells: Python ingestion into PostgreSQL, ~800K rule-based findings,
			reporting in Grafana, Power BI and a Next.js front end. Site configuration scripts went from
			~2 hours to ~20 minutes per site.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>AI engineering applied to a domain I know deeply.</strong> Post-Graduate in AI &amp;
			Machine Learning (UT Austin), now focused on LLM agents, tool calling, tracing and
			evaluation. Knowing what a wrong SMTC window or a bad B1 threshold looks like is what lets me
			design the right tools and judge whether the agent is actually right.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>What I've built</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<a href="https://github.com/codeviloria/5g-nsa-agent">5G NSA troubleshooting agent</a>{' '}
			(open source): a tool-calling agent that diagnoses EN-DC problems on local models, built in
			plain Python and with LangChain, debugged with LangSmith traces. Four trace-driven iterations
			took it to every root cause found, with no unsupported recommendations, in both test
			scenarios. <a href="/blog/5g-nsa-agent-tool-calling-gemma4/">Read the write-up</a>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>RAN field-validation tools</strong>: Python tools and a Telegram bot that pull alarms,
			KPIs and configuration from Huawei OSS for a cell or coordinate in one message. Next step:
			turning them into tools for an agent.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Home AI lab</strong>: CPU-only servers running local LLMs and embedding models with
			Ollama, RAG proofs of concept and MCP servers built with FastMCP.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>What I'm learning now</h2>
	<p>
		LangChain Academy (<em>Introduction to LangChain</em> completed) on the way to the{' '}
		<strong>LangChain Certified Agent Engineer (LCAE)</strong> exam; LangGraph, agent memory and
		evaluation, the Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol; and fine-tuning open-weight models with LoRA/QLoRA
		on Kaggle GPUs. Each topic ends up as a project and a post here.
	</p>

	<h2>How this blog works</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Runs on <strong>local models</strong> (Ollama), with no paid APIs.</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Traced and measured</strong>: what failed and what fixed it, not just the final demo.
		</li>
		<li>
			The <strong>code is public</strong>. Network data is simulated to reproduce real-world
			failures; no operator data is used.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Let's talk</h2>
	<p>
		I'm <strong>open to Telecom AI, RAN automation and AI engineering roles</strong>, remote or with
		relocation. If you're building AI for networks, or need someone who speaks both RAN and LLMs,
		reach me on <a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a> or see the code on{' '}
		<a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>.
	</p>
</Layout>
